WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:05.520
Futurama is a show that grounds itself in&nbsp;
the absurd, and the inhabitants of the year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:10.800
3000 tend to play by different rules to&nbsp;
the rest of us. Still, there have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:16.880
a few occasions on which the cast of Futurama&nbsp;
have crossed a line — even by their standards.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:22.960
Lying in order to avoid punishment is an&nbsp;
ill-advised but understandable indiscretion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:26.480
And it's certainly something of&nbsp;
an overreach when Planet Express'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:29.760
robotic ne'er-do-well Bender tells authorities on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:35.840
Chapek 9 that he personally slaughtered a&nbsp;
million billion humans to avoid their wrath.

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:41.360
In Season 1's "Fear of a Bot Planet,"&nbsp;
a delivery to the zealously anti-human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:47.280
Chapek 9 goes south when Bender is arrested&nbsp;
for his association with Earthlings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:53.760
Diabolical monster that he is, Bender exaggerates&nbsp;
his history of mass murder in order to exploit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:59.920
the population's bigotry and propel himself to&nbsp;
celebrity status. Of course, Bender is a liar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:05.440
who hasn't killed a million billion humans. In&nbsp;
fact, most of the stories about humankind that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:12.560
tells the gullible mechanical citizens of Chapek&nbsp;
9 are lifted from vampire and werewolf legends.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:16.560
"Is it true they bite your neck and suck your&nbsp;
transmission fluid and then you become a human?"

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:17.840
"Sure, why not."

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:23.120
Ultimately, Bender drops the act to rescue&nbsp;
his co-worker pals Fry and Leela from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:29.280
execution at the hands of the Council of Robot&nbsp;
Elders. Once the Chapek 9 robots realize that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:34.640
the human Fry and the human-ish Leela have&nbsp;
brought a large box of precious lug nuts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:39.520
they abandon their anti-human bigotry. But&nbsp;
it's fair to say that, had Bender had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:44.080
left to his own devices, things might&nbsp;
have turned out more than a little badly.

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:49.760
Lock up your pets, folks — Bender&nbsp;
Bending Rodriguez is in town.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:55.040
Season 4's "Jurassic Bark" sits atop&nbsp;
many lists of Futurama's best episodes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.040 --> 00:02:00.720
and for good reason. It's the heartwarming&nbsp;
tale of Fry discovering the fossilized remains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:07.280
of his 20th century canine companion, Seymour.&nbsp;
Ultimately, Fry decides against bringing Seymour&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:13.120
back via cloning to join him in the 31st&nbsp;
century. Before Fry comes to that decision,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:19.840
however, Bender throws the fossilized pup into&nbsp;
a nearby lava pit in a fit of violent jealousy.

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:25.680
And sure, Bender pulls the pooch out of danger&nbsp;
once he realizes he's totally destroyed Fry's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:32.800
soul by giving in to misguided envy. But still,&nbsp;
what kind of demented sociopath tries to off a dog&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:38.480
with molten lava? Even when you take into account&nbsp;
the fact that robots don't have souls, this is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:43.280
pretty crummy thing to do. And it's hardly the&nbsp;
first time Bender's eyes have turned green,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:50.080
too: for similar reasons, he flushes Nibbler town&nbsp;
a toilet in Season 2's "I Second That Emotion."

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:54.800
"Aren't you upset at all? How would you&nbsp;
feel if I flushed Fry down the toilet?"

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:56.160
"Only one way to find out."

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:00.160
What else can you say? The dude really hates pets.

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:06.320
Bender's an amoral kleptomaniac on the best&nbsp;
of days, but his friendship with Fry makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:12.160
him look more like a lovable rascal… most&nbsp;
of the time, at least. Sometimes, it simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:18.560
provides Bender with a rationalization for his&nbsp;
most overtly supervillain-ish machinations. .

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:22.880
At the conclusion of the 2008 special&nbsp;
The Beast With A Billion Backs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:29.440
Bender finds himself resenting Fry for spending&nbsp;
so much time with Yivo, a space god who is also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:36.080
romancing every living creature in the universe&nbsp;
at once. Outraged, Bender travels to Robot Hell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:42.960
to recruit an army of demon robots to rise up and&nbsp;
conquer Earth. The Robot Devil says no dice on an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:49.280
army, unless Bender trades his first-born child&nbsp;
— a deal that Bender really has no problem with.

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:54.320
"Wow, that was pretty brutal&nbsp;
even by my standards."

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:55.760
"No backsies."

00:03:55.760 --> 00:04:01.760
When Bender springs his unholy invasion, Fry&nbsp;
informs him that all living creatures are moving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:08.000
off their planets to live on Yivo's head, which&nbsp;
is the basis for humanity's conception of Heaven.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:13.840
Not long after, still missing Fry, Bender&nbsp;
leads his army of the damned to raze Heaven.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:19.200
In the end, Bender's invasion spurs&nbsp;
Yivo to break up with the universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:25.680
cruelly tearing every living thing away from its&nbsp;
blissful salvation. In short? Not cool, Bender.

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:30.720
How far would you go to keep your pride?&nbsp;
How about the pride of your entire home&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:36.000
planet? In Season 3's "Time Keeps On&nbsp;
Slippin'," the Harlem Globetrotters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:41.680
fly in from the Globetrotter homeworld&nbsp;
to challenge Earth to a basketball game.

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:43.200
"What happens if we lose?"

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:48.640
"Nothing! There's nothing at stake and&nbsp;
no threat, beyond the shame of defeat."

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:53.200
Of course, you wouldn't expect this to&nbsp;
bother Professor Hubert J. Farnsworth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:58.000
As the owner of Planet Express and the man&nbsp;
directly responsible for the appalling and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:04.000
needless deaths of numerous ex-employees, he&nbsp;
should already be completely numb to shame.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:09.440
Rather than allow a ragtag squad of Earthlings&nbsp;
to graciously lose an exhibition game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:15.920
Farnsworth sends Fry, Leela, and Bender to the&nbsp;
far reaches of the galaxy to collect unstable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:22.800
chroniton particles. Upon their return, he exposes&nbsp;
baby mutant supermen to the ominous materials in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:28.720
order to age them into an adult basketball&nbsp;
team in an insanely brief period of time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:33.120
But Farnsworth's reckless and&nbsp;
bafflingly unnecessary behavior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:38.560
causes massive damage to the time-space&nbsp;
continuum. The linear sequence of events&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:44.960
begins flashing forward at unpredictable&nbsp;
intervals, imperiling the very matrix of reality.

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:51.120
Luckily, Farnsworth proves more than capable of&nbsp;
undoing his own mistake. He and the Globetrotters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:56.960
join forces and eventually figure out a&nbsp;
solution, bringing space-time back to normal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.960 --> 00:06:01.760
You'd hope he'd have learned his lesson after&nbsp;
that, but come on — let's not kid ourselves.

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:05.280
In the highly partisan era in which we live,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:11.680
plenty of folks will relate to Zapp Brannigan's&nbsp;
unhinged contempt for the concept of neutrality.

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:17.120
"With enemies you know where you stand, but&nbsp;
with neutrals? Who knows? It sickens me."

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:22.480
In Season 2's "Brannigan, Begin Again,"&nbsp;
Zapp's hatred for neutrality spills over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:28.400
into delusional paranoia. The Democratic Order&nbsp;
of Planets invites Zapp as the guest of honor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:33.520
at the ribbon-cutting ceremony for the new&nbsp;
DOOP headquarters in the Neutral Zone. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:39.600
when the Planet Express crew arrives to deliver&nbsp;
the oversized novelty scissors Zapp needs to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:45.520
perform his ribbon-cutting duties, Zapp arrests&nbsp;
them immediately on suspicion of neutrality.

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:49.840
Because committing civil rights&nbsp;
violations against the Planet Express crew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:55.520
proves time-consuming and distracting, Zapp&nbsp;
decides to save himself a few minutes by using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.520 --> 00:07:01.760
his spaceship's laser — set to "Hyperdeath"&nbsp;
mode — to cut the DOOP HQ ribbon from space.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:06.880
And so concludes the brief but noteworthy&nbsp;
existence of the new DOOP headquarters.

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:07.920
"Whoopsie daisy."

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:14.560
Zapp's entire presence on Futurama depends&nbsp;
on him making breathtakingly poor choices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:20.480
that force the folks around him to mop up his&nbsp;
messes. But blowing up DOOP HQ for essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:26.000
no reason at all is going much too far, even&nbsp;
with his track record of senseless mayhem.

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:32.320
The version of Richard Nixon who appears in&nbsp;
Futurama might not be as rotten as the actual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:38.560
37th president of the United States, but his&nbsp;
capacity for destruction is still very impressive.

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:44.160
In Season 2's "A Head in the Polls," Richard&nbsp;
Nixon decides to get back in the political game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:48.880
and run for president of Earth. Like many&nbsp;
other celebrities and historical figures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:54.160
in the 31st century, Nixon's existence is&nbsp;
limited to that of a floating head in a jar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:59.520
due to the decomposed condition of his old body.&nbsp;
Much of the episode's story revolves around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:06.000
Nixon's technically legal use of Bender's robot&nbsp;
body. But once Bender reclaims his torso, arms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:11.840
and legs, Nixon simply acquires a much larger&nbsp;
body equipped with devastating weapons systems.

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:14.000
"Nixon's back!"

00:08:16.640 --> 00:08:22.320
This Gundam-esque Nixon locks down the robot&nbsp;
vote and becomes the new president of Earth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:27.040
Still bitter and crazy from his infamous&nbsp;
near-impeachment and resignation over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:31.280
Watergate scandal, Nixon embarks upon&nbsp;
a rampage of death and destruction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:36.560
throughout the city of Washington D.C. The&nbsp;
nation of Earth may never fully recover.

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:43.040
When a man lives his first 20-something years as&nbsp;
a dangerously broke pizza delivery technician,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.040 --> 00:08:47.520
he's bound to cut loose if his luck&nbsp;
ever swings in the opposite direction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:52.160
So it's understandable why Fry shamelessly&nbsp;
flaunts his wealth when the interest on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:58.080
his thousand-year-old bank account makes him a&nbsp;
billionaire in Season 1's "A Fishful of Dollars."

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:03.680
Now, plenty of people will tell you that taxidermy&nbsp;
is in pretty poor taste, and collecting skeletons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:09.600
really isn't much less creepy. And sure enough,&nbsp;
when Fry pays $10,000 at an auction for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:15.920
skeleton of stalwart television actor Ted Danson,&nbsp;
you've got to admit he's kind of crossing a line.

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:20.560
Fry's newfound ocean of cash and&nbsp;
pre-existing '90s nostalgia coalesces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:25.280
into a turn of events that introduces&nbsp;
the evil company known as MomCorp.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:30.800
Danson's bones are only mentioned in a one-off&nbsp;
gag, as Fry says he needs them because he's got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:35.760
an idea for a sitcom. But the fact that Fry&nbsp;
thinks treats the remains of a once-living&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:40.720
person like this demonstrates exactly how&nbsp;
far down the rabbit hole he has fallen.

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:46.320
Anyone will tell you how much it sucks to&nbsp;
miss out on the finale of your favorite show.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:52.960
But when Lrrr, ruler of the planet Omicron Persei&nbsp;
8, is prevented from watching the last episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.960 --> 00:09:58.640
of Single Female Lawyer after a technical&nbsp;
glitch on Earth, he overreacts just a tad.

00:09:58.640 --> 00:09:59.280
"I'm beginning to think there'll&nbsp;
be no forced mating at all."

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:04.720
The trajectory of events depicted in Season 1's&nbsp;
"When Aliens Attack" is set in motion before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:10.800
the opening credits. As Fry often ends up at the&nbsp;
center of universe-altering events in Futurama,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:15.920
it tracks that he was the one who spilled&nbsp;
beer on the late-'90s Fox control panel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:21.280
eventually cutting off the broadcast Omicronians&nbsp;
who are watching 1,000 light years away.

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:26.720
To extract his revenge and enforce&nbsp;
his will, Lrrr swoops down to Earth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:33.200
and immediately destroys the Eiffel Tower, the&nbsp;
Great Sphinx of Giza, the Leaning Tower of Pisa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:38.880
and other prominent Earth landmarks. He pulls&nbsp;
off all that in pretty rapid succession, too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:44.240
though that's mostly because they've all been&nbsp;
relocated to the same beach in New New York.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:50.320
Independence Day, eat your heart out. Anyway,&nbsp;
Lrrr gets to see his program by the episode's end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:56.560
mostly thanks to a little creativity by the Planet&nbsp;
Express crew. Talk about a temper tantrum, though.

00:10:57.440 --> 00:11:02.960
Over time, societies change, and behaviour&nbsp;
considered taboo in one era might be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.960 --> 00:11:07.120
acceptable during another. But there are&nbsp;
some things that cross a line no matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:12.560
where or when you come from. Graverobbing&nbsp;
definitely belongs in that category,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:16.000
especially if that grave&nbsp;
belongs to a family member.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:22.720
In Season 3's "The Luck of the Fryrish," Fry is&nbsp;
horrified to discover a 21st century man named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:29.360
Philip J. Fry — who looks just like him — took&nbsp;
possession of his long-lost seven-leaf clover.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:35.840
Worse still, this imposter apparently became&nbsp;
a globally-famous astronaut and rock star.

00:11:35.840 --> 00:11:42.000
Fry assumes this paragon of human achievement must&nbsp;
be his brother Yancy, who would've had access to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:47.920
the clover and knowledge of its near-supernatural&nbsp;
powers. While in the process of digging up what he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.920 --> 00:11:53.360
thinks is his elder sibling's corpse, however,&nbsp;
Fry notices the gravestone's inscription.

00:11:54.080 --> 00:12:00.080
"Here lies Philip J. Fry, named for&nbsp;
his uncle, to carry on his spirit."

00:12:00.960 --> 00:12:06.560
This happens just in time for Bender to break&nbsp;
open the coffin and snag the lucky clover.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.560 --> 00:12:12.240
It's an embarrassing mix-up, to be sure, but it's&nbsp;
also a lesson to the rest of us that grave-robbing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.240 --> 00:12:16.640
is kind of a no-no… no matter what your&nbsp;
robotic best friend might tell you.

00:12:17.600 --> 00:12:23.520
Fry transgresses the boundaries of decency in&nbsp;
a whole new way in Season 3's "Roswell That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:30.240
Ends Well." In this episode, Fry accidentally&nbsp;
sends the Planet Express ship back to the 1940s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.240 --> 00:12:36.960
where it crashes in Roswell, New Mexico. Plenty&nbsp;
of hijinks ensue: Zoidberg gets vivisected,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.960 --> 00:12:43.680
Farnsworth and Leela go shopping, and Bender&nbsp;
becomes… whatever this is. But Fry's dalliance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:48.960
with history is the most impressive of all.&nbsp;
During his relatively short time in the past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.960 --> 00:12:54.560
Fry manages to accidentally kill his grandfather,&nbsp;
deliberately sleep with his grandmother, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.560 --> 00:13:02.134
accidentally impregnate her with the fetus that&nbsp;
eventually becomes Fry's father, Yancy Fry Sr.

00:13:08.240 --> 00:13:14.320
The events of this episode reverberate throughout&nbsp;
the mythology of Futurama. In fact, Fry's status&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.320 --> 00:13:20.160
as his own grandfather is, unbeknownst to everyone&nbsp;
but Nibbler, the real reason why he's in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.160 --> 00:13:26.720
future in the first place. The alterations made&nbsp;
to Fry's brain by doing the nasty in the pasty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.720 --> 00:13:33.040
later makes him the ideal candidate to defeat&nbsp;
the evil species known as brain-spawn and save&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.040 --> 00:13:39.280
the entire universe. It also makes him a total&nbsp;
doofus, but hey — you win some, you lose some.

